Workspace for Ethernet Goodie

---------------------------------- 
Waking up the Ethernet:
----------------------------------

Ether wakeup. "initializes Etherworld data structures"

---------------------------------- 
Shutting down the Ethernet:
----------------------------------

Smalltalk releaseExternalViews. "closes all connections"

Ether kill. "releases Etherworld data structures"

----------------------------------
Accessing an IFS:
----------------------------------

RemoteDisk _ IFSLeafFileDirectory directory: nil directoryName: 'IFSName'.
RemoteDisk login: 'userName' password: 'userPassword'.
RemoteDisk connect: '' password: ''.

RemoteDisk login.
RemoteDisk connect.

----------------------------------
Chat using Telnet protocol:
----------------------------------

Chatter chatTo: 'IFSName'.

----------------------------------
BSP protocol debugging:
----------------------------------

ByteStreamSocket debug: true.
ByteStreamSocket initializeLogFile: Transcript.
ByteStreamSocket initializeLogFile: (FileStream fileNamed: 'BSP.log').
ByteStreamSocket debug: false.

----------------------------------
FTP protocol testing:
----------------------------------

RemoteDisk filesMatching: '<dirName>subDir>*.st'.

Smalltalk at: #Test put: FileTransferSocket new.
Test createConnectionTo: 'IFSName'. "establishes a connection"
Test destroyConnection. "breaks the connection"

Test errorMessage. "returns last error message"
Test errorCode. "returns last error code"

	"Retrieve"
Test retrieve: '((User-Name userName)(User-Password userPassword)(Server-Filename <dirName>remoteFileName))'
	to: (FileStream fileNamed: 'localFileName').

	"Store"
Test store: '((User-Name userName)(User-Password userPassword)(Server-Filename <dirName>remoteFileName)(Type text))'
	from: (FileStream oldFileNamed: 'localFileName').

	"Rename"
Test renameFrom: '((User-Name userName)(User-Password userPassword)(Server-Filename <dirName>oldFileName))'
	to: '((Server-Filename <dirName>newFileName))'.

	"Delete"
Smalltalk at: #Result put: nil.
Result _ ((Test delete: '((User-Name userName)(User-Password userPassword)(Server-Filename <dirName>fileName*)(Desired-Property Server-Filename))')
	collect:
		[:propList | propList at: 'Server-Filename']).
Result. "printIt"

	"Enumerate"
Smalltalk at: #Result put: nil.
Result _ ((Test enumerate: '((User-Name userName)(User-Password userPassword)(Server-Filename <dirName>fileName*)(Desired-Property Server-Filename)(version H))')
	collect:
		[:propList | propList at: 'Server-Filename']).
Result. "printIt"

---------------------------------- 
FTP Documentation:
----------------------------------

[From "Pup File Transfer Protocol Specification--5th Edition"]

FTP Property List Format

   A property list is a parenthesized list consisting of one or more
   properties, each of which is also parenthesized:
	((property-1 value1)(property-2 value2))

Mandatory Properties

   (Server-Filename fileName)
	In commands from user to server (such as Store, Retrieve, and
	Enumerate), specifies the name of the file to be manipulated.

   (Type Text|Binary)
	Specifies the type of data to be transferred (no default).

   (End-of-Line-Convention CR|CRLF|Transparent)
	Specifies representation for end-of-line in files transmitted
	as type Text (default is CR).

   (Byte-Size decimal-number)
	Specifies the logical byte size of files of type Binary (no
	default; meaningless and ignored if the type is Text).

   (Device device-name)
   (Directory directory-name)
   (Name-Body name-body)
   (Version decimal-number)
	Specifies the separate components of a filename in a manner
	intended to facilitate transfer of files between hosts whose
	file systems lack one or more of these concepts or have
	different conventions for punctuating them. The values of the
	properties should not include the surrounding punctuation
	characters.

Optional Properties

   (Size decimal-number)
	Specifies the number of bytes in the file. (These bytes are
	of the size specified by the Byte-Size property). The value
	is not required to be correct (it may be provided as a hint).

   (User-Name user-name)
	Specifies a legal user name on the server system, for the
	purpose of access checking and accounting, if required.

   (User-Password password)
	Supplies the user password, if required for logging in under
	the specified User-Name.

   (User-Account account-designator)
	Specifies the user "account" (in the Tenex sense), if
	required. If this is not specified, the user is logged in
	under his default account (assuming there is one).

   (Connect-Name directory-name)
	Specifies that the user is to be "connected" (in the Tenex
	sense) to the specified directory, i.e., to be given
	temporary owner-like access to that directory. The default
	connected directory is the user's login directory.

   (Connect-Password password)
	Supplies the password for connecting to a directory, if
	required.

   (Creation-Date date-and-time)
   (Write-Date date-and-time)
   (Read-Date date-and-time)
	Supplies the respective date property of the file. The syntax
	of date-and-time is "dd-mmm-yy hh:mm:ss zzz", where zzz is a
	standard North American time zone (e.g., PST, EDT), GMT, or a
	sign followed by a numeric time zone (e.g., +3 or +3:00,
	meaning 3 hours west of Greenwich).

   (Author user-name)
	Supplies the name of the user who created the file.

   (Checksum decimal-number)
	Supplies an optional checksum over the file. The checksum is
	computed over the file's data in the form in which it flows
	over the BSP stream, but treating it as a sequence of 16-bit
	words (with the left byte of each word first, and with the
	leftover byte assumed to be zero if the file consists of an
	odd number of bytes).

   (Desired-Property property-name)
	In a command from user to server, requests that the server
	supply the specified property in all file property lists it
	generates in response to that command. (That is,
	property-name should be one of Author, Byte-Size, Checksum,
	Creation-Date, Device, Directory, Name-Body, Read-Date,
	Server-FileName, Size, Type, Version, or Write-Date). If no
	Desired-Property property is present in the property list of
	a file-related command sent from user to server, the server
	must send all known properties of each file enumerated.
